<h2>San Marcos Codes and the Rebuild</h2>
<p>The City of San Marcos has enforced the 2021 International Codes, including the IRC, IBC, IECC, and IFC, along with the 2020 National Electrical Code, since April 1, 2023 under Ordinance 2023-09. A local amendment requires that all foundations be designed by a licensed engineer who submits a sealed letter, which can matter when a fire damages a slab or requires a new foundation. Permits run through the city <a href="https://www.sanmarcostx.gov/1000/Permit-Center" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Permit Center</a>.</p>
<p>Outside city limits, Hays County does not issue residential building permits, though it applies the 2018 International Fire Code to new commercial and multifamily buildings. Kyle, Buda, Wimberley, and other nearby cities have their own permit offices. Code requirements do not automatically mean the insurer pays for upgrades; that depends on ordinance or law coverage. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page and <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-san-marcos/">codes and insurance repairs article</a> go deeper.</p>

<h2>Common Problems on Fire Claims</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Clean-versus-replace disputes</strong> for drywall, framing, and contents exposed to heat and smoke.</li>
<li><strong>Underestimated contents</strong> because the inventory was rushed or incomplete.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation</strong> on contents and building materials under actual cash value terms. See our <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">RCV versus ACV guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Living expense cutoffs</strong> before the home is actually ready.</li>
<li><strong>Missed systems</strong> like ductwork, electrical runs, and appliances that look intact but were exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Building a Contents Inventory That Holds Up</h2>
<p>For many households, the personal property list is the hardest part of a fire claim. A practical way to approach it:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Go room by room.</strong> Start with one room and list everything in it, including closets, drawers, and cabinets.</li>
<li><strong>Use what you already have.</strong> Phone photos, social media pictures, holiday videos, bank and credit card statements, and online order histories can help you reconstruct what you owned and when you bought it.</li>
<li><strong>Record the basics for each item:</strong> description, brand or model where known, quantity, approximate age, and a current replacement price.</li>
<li><strong>Do not discard items early.</strong> Keep damaged property until it has been photographed and the insurer has had a chance to see it, unless it is a safety hazard.</li>
<li><strong>Separate cleanable from non-salvageable.</strong> Restoration companies can often clean hard goods and some textiles; items that cannot be restored go on the replacement list.</li>
</ol>
<p>A complete, organized inventory tends to move faster and draws fewer follow-up questions than a partial one. It also makes depreciation easier to review line by line.</p>

<h2>How the Fire Claim Process Works With Us</h2>
<ol>
<li>A free consultation to understand the loss and review your policy.</li>
<li>A written agreement, with fees disclosed and a 72-hour right to rescind.</li>
<li>Detailed inspection and documentation of the structure, systems, and contents.</li>
<li>Estimates, inventories, and living-expense summaries submitted to the insurer.</li>
<li>Ongoing negotiation and follow-up, including <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplements</a> when more damage is uncovered during demolition.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our article <a href="/after-the-flames-insurance-adjuster/">After the Flames</a> walks through the first weeks after a fire in more detail.</p>

<h2>Additional Living Expenses: Keeping the Paper Trail</h2>
<p>Loss of use coverage generally pays the increase in your living costs, not your normal expenses. If your family now pays for a rental house, extra mileage to school and work, laundry, or meals out because the kitchen is gone, those increases may be claimable. Keep receipts in one place, write down your normal monthly costs for comparison, and ask the insurer in writing how long the coverage period is expected to last so you are not surprised by a cutoff while repairs are still underway.</p>

<h2>When to Call a Public Adjuster After a Fire</h2>
<p>The earlier the better, because early documentation shapes the whole claim. A public adjuster in San Marcos can be especially helpful when the loss is large, when you are displaced and overwhelmed, when contents are extensive, or when the insurer&rsquo;s estimate does not match contractor pricing. Business owners can also see our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page. Request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a> when you are ready.</p>
